  "updated": "2026-09-02T11:22:50.420683+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nOngoing convection across southern Minnesota and central Wisconsin \nwill continue to progress east-northeast through the morning, with \nprimary convective concerns holding across northern Lake Huron, \nnorthern lower MI, and the U.P. Very moist low-level conditions \nremain in place across SE MI with surface dewpoints in the low 70s, \nwhich will support intermittent stratus and isolated showers through\nthe morning. Showers or an isolated thunderstorm will be most \nfavorable along or north of M59 this morning. \n\nFor this afternoon, any lingering low clouds are expected to erode \nquickly with enhanced boundary-layer mixing. With the projected \nconvection and thicker cloud canopies holding north of the cwa, h850\ntemperatures aoa 20C, and some early day sunshine, daytime highs \npeak in the low 90s across a greater portion of the cwa. Mixing may \ntemper the dew points slightly during peak heating, but the high \nboundary layer moisture should still support heat indices aoa 100\ndegrees across southern Michigan. This will be most favorable from \nthe MI/OH border and up through the M-59 corridor, noting the usual \nenhanced potential across the urban Metro corridor. As a result, a \nHeat Advisory is now in effect from Noon through 8 PM EDT. Conditions\nstill remain hot and muggy north of the advisory zones with heat \nindices ranging in the mid to upper 90s. The higher potential for mid\nto high level clouds and earlier arrival of potential showers and \nstorms (more below) precludes the issuance of heat advisories farther\nnorth. \n\nAttention then turns to the potential influence of a convectively \ngenerated outflow from the activity that is projected to traverse \nacross northern Michigan this morning. This boundary would be slated \nto arrive across the Tri-Cities and Thumb late this afternoon and \nevening, coincident with peak daytime heating. MLCAPE values between \n2500-3000 J/kg along with strong kinematics characterized by bulk \nshear values of 40-45 knots will be likely by the afternoon. Any CI \ngenerated off an outflow/pseudo cold front could result in organized \nconvection capable of generating strong to severe thunderstorms. The \ngreatest confidence for this scenario resides across the Thumb where \nseveral hi-res solutions depict a more pronounced southward-moving \nboundary. This potential may extend west into the Tri-Cities for\ninitial development before progressing southward toward the I-69 \ncorridor and Port Huron through the evening. \n\nIf strong to severe thunderstorms develop, storm mode may briefly \nstart as semi-discrete but would likely quickly amalgamate to multi-\ncell clusters or a linear mode. Strong 1-3 and 1-6km shear values of\n30-40 knots could support supercell development with any initial \ndiscrete formation under modest streamwise vorticity within the \nmidlevels. The likely hazards would likely be damaging wind gusts \nwith any bowing segments from cold pool generation or microbursts \ngiven deep layer CAPE. Large hail is also possible noting the CAPE \nvalues and strong mid-level lapse rates. Last, ML output does \nhighlight chances for a tornado, most favorable within the Thumb. \nUncertainty is higher with tornadogenesis. Again any initial discrete\ndevelopment would would make this possible noting 0-1km shear around\n20 knots, but with current thinking hedged towards multi- \ncell/linear, ambient shear profile would be altered. Some guidance \nsuggests secondary boundaries capable of propagating farther south \ninto the evening through the Metro region. However uncertainty is \nhigher in southern extent of any convection as timing of this \ncoincides with loss of daytime heating. Overall, SWODY1 places all of\nSE MI in a slight risk for severe weather with all storm hazards \npossible. \n\nSlightly cooler temperatures projected for Thursday but very humid \nconditions remain as dew points hold in the 70s. Quasi-zonal flow \nushers in warm and humid conditions into the Great Lakes from the \nPlains, which will again support the chance for showers and \nthunderstorms tomorrow. SWODY2 clips a portion of southern Michigan \nfor a Marginal risk for severe weather. Persistence forecast holds \ninto Friday, with the overall pattern starting to breakdown into the \nweekend as ridge amplification commences across the Plains.",
